¹ Multiplier hierarchy is derived from injury incidence data presented in 'Decoding NFL Injuries' (which identifies WR, S, LB as high-frequency) and 'Epidemiology of Injuries Identified at the NFL Scouting Combine' (which highlights DBs as high-risk).

### 3.4. Player-Specific Risk Factors

After establishing the risk inherent to the play and the player's position, the final layer of personalization is applied based on the individual player's physical state. These factors, which influence injury risk, are distinct from the factors that modify injury recovery.

Table 3.4.1: Player-Specific Risk Multipliers | Factor | Rating/Age Band | Injury Risk Multiplier | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | Age | 30-32 | 1.05x | | | 33+ | 1.15x | | Durability Rating | 90-99 | 0.9x | | | 80-89 | 0.95x | | Medical Staff Rating | 90-99 | 0.95x | | | 80-89 | 0.98x |

Finally, a dynamic Fatigue/Wear multiplier will be included in the calculation. This value represents the cumulative physical toll on a player, increasing their injury risk as their energy level decreases throughout a game and over the course of a season, directly linking this system to the engine's overarching Wear and Tear System.

This probabilistic model provides a robust framework for determining when injuries occur. The final section details how this system will be integrated into the simulation's core game loop.

## 4. System Integration and Implementation

A clean and deterministic integration strategy is paramount to maintaining the stability and replayability of the simulation engine. The new injury logic will be encapsulated within two new functions in injury_system.py and will be invoked by the main simulation orchestrator immediately after each play is fully resolved.

### 4.1. Post-Play Evaluation

The function evaluate_post_play_injuries(play_context, players_on_field) will serve as the sole entry point for the injury evaluation process within the game loop. Critically, this function is executed after a play's outcome has been finalized. This sequence is mandatory to ensure simulation determinism; an injury event must not influence the state of the play in which it is generated.

The function's internal logic will proceed as follows:

1. Iterate through all players who were on the field and involved in the play.
2. For each player, call the compute_play_injury_probability function, passing the relevant play context and player data to calculate their specific injury chance for that play.
3. Use the simulation's seeded Random Number Generator (RNG) to perform a check against the calculated probability.
4. If an injury occurs, generate a severity value and pass it to the existing determine_injury_outcome(severity, player_profile) function, which will handle the assignment of injury type, body part, recovery timeline, and trigger checks for setbacks or permanent degradation.
5. Return a list of any generated InjuryEvent objects, which will be applied to the player statuses before the simulation proceeds to the next play.

### 4.2. Configuration and Testing

All base probabilities and multipliers detailed in Section 3 will be centralized in configuration files. This allows for straightforward tuning and balancing without requiring code changes, facilitating rapid iteration based on simulation results.

A critical requirement is that all tests must be deterministic. This will be achieved by injecting a seeded Random Number Generator (RNG) into the evaluate_post_play_injuries function during test runs. By providing a known seed, the outcome of the probability check becomes repeatable, allowing for consistent and reliable validation of the injury logic in the pytest environment.

